=","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"~","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"logit[","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"9","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":"];","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"      ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"if","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" (p ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"==","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"4'd7","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":") busy ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"<=","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"1'b0","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":";","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"      ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"else","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":"           p    ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"<=","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" p ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"+","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"4'd1","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":";","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"    ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"end","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true}],[{"text":"endmodule","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true}]],"bugLine":45,"fixLine":"decision <= (logit > 0);"},{"id":"conv-predict-edge","itemId":"conv-layer","role":"core","promptHtml":"<code class=\"nobreak\">conv_row</code> from lesson 11.1, with the weights 2, −1 and 3 and the bias −1, works along the row 81 (hexadecimal), one position a cycle; the taps are not drawn. In cycle 0, <code class=\"nobreak\">decision</code> still holds the last row's last decision, 0. Set <code class=\"nobreak\">decision</code> for every cycle.","explanationHtml":"<p>81 is 1000 0001: <code class=\"nobreak\">x[0]</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">x[7]</code> are 1. Position 0's taps are the padding's 0, 1 and 0: −2, decide 0. Position 1 has a 1 on its left: 1, decide 1. Positions 2 to 5 see no 1: −1. Position 6 has a 1 on its right: 2, decide 1. Position 7's right tap is the padding's 0, so it sees only its own 1: −2. Each decision shows a cycle later: 0 0 1 0 0 0 0 1 0.</p>","type":"waveform","wave":{"id":"conv-edge","source":"hdl/level-11/conv_row/conv_row_tb.sv","timescale":"1ps","period":10000,"periodText":"10 ns","unit":"cycle","cycles":9,"signals":[{"label":"clk","path":"conv_row_tb.clk","width":1,"role":"clock","changes":[[0,"1"],[0.5,"0"],[1,"1"],[1.5,"0"],[2,"1"],[2.5,"0"],[3,"1"],[3.5,"0"],[4,"1"],[4.5,"0"],[5,"1"],[5.5,"0"],[6,"1"],[6.5,"0"],[7,"1"],[7.5,"0"],[8,"1"],[8.5,"0"]]},{"label":"x[7:0]","path":"conv_row_tb.x","width":8,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"10000001"]]},{"label":"p[3:0]","path":"conv_row_tb.p","width":4,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0000"],[1,"0001"],[2,"0010"],[3,"0011"],[4,"0100"],[5,"0101"],[6,"0110"],[7,"0111"]]},{"label":"decision","path":"conv_row_tb.decision","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[2,"1"],[3,"0"],[7,"1"],[8,"0"]],"samples":["0","0","1","0","0","0","0","1","0"]}]},"answer":"decision"},{"id":"conv-choice-reach","itemId":"conv-layer","role":"core","promptHtml":"One output of the decoder's network depends on a square of the input around its position. The network has three layers, each with an 11 × 11 kernel. How big is that square?","explanationHtml":"<p>An 11 × 11 kernel reaches five positions past its centre. The output reads layer two up to five positions away, each of those reads layer one up to five further, and layer one reads the input five further still: 15 on every side, so 1 + 2 × 15 = 31. Counting 11 for each layer counts the shared centre three times. 19 × 19 is how the input is stored, padding included, not how far an output sees.</p>","type":"choice","options":["31 × 31: each layer reaches five positions further on every side.","11 × 11: the size of one kernel.","33 × 33: three windows of 11, side by side.","19 × 19: the size of the stored input."],"correct":0}]